arrangements                          : 800
spread regime  : {'W>8mu': 800}

NOTE ON SCOPE. Section 4.6's claim -- that at the global start every SPEED gap reads
>= 1/8 so only the wrap can be short -- needs W <= 8*mu. A speed gap reads a_k/(8*mu),
which is a POSITION difference mod 1, so once W > 8*mu the readings exceed a lap, the
runners have crossed, and speed-adjacency is no longer position-adjacency. Section 4.6
says exactly this: 'Beyond W = 8*mu the short gap moves into the interior as runners
cross.' The counts below mix both regimes and are split afterwards.

at the global start t = 1/(8*mu), number of SHORT gaps (<1/8):
   0 short :     2  (0.2%)
   1 short :     7  (0.9%)
   2 short :    59  (7.4%)
   3 short :   173  (21.6%)
   4 short :   290  (36.2%)
   5 short :   213  (26.6%)
   6 short :    52  (6.5%)
   7 short :     4  (0.5%)

runners already LONELY at the global start (both flanks >= 1/8):
   0 lonely:    98  (12.2%)
   1 lonely:   226  (28.2%)
   2 lonely:   259  (32.4%)
   3 lonely:   128  (16.0%)
   4 lonely:    67  (8.4%)
   5 lonely:    13  (1.6%)
   6 lonely:     7  (0.9%)
   8 lonely:     2  (0.2%)

